#: ../../object-types.rst:1
msgid "Declaring object types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:4
msgid "A project may need names that the standard domains do not know. Declare those object types in TOML. Guidedog can then create targets, index entries, and roles without loading a Python extension. The declaration states a syntax, not executable behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:8
msgid "Targets: ``add_crossref_type``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:18
msgid "``.. setting:: DEBUG`` then makes a target with the id ``setting-DEBUG`` and an index entry, and ``:setting:`DEBUG``` links to it, as in Sphinx. ``index`` is Sphinx's ``indextemplate``: its type (``single``, ``pair``, ``triple``, ``see``, ``seealso``) before the first colon, and the entry after it, with ``%s`` the name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:23
msgid "Descriptions: ``add_object_type``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:36
msgid "``.. django-admin:: migrate [app_label]`` then describes the command like any object: a signature, content, an id (``django-admin-migrate``), and an index entry. In Sphinx a ``parse_node`` function may read the signature; its usual work is set by fields instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:41
msgid "``name``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:42
msgid "``\"whole\"`` (the default): the object's name is its whole signature. ``\"first-word\"``: the name is the signature up to its first space (``pdb``'s ``b(reak) [lineno]`` is named ``b(reak)``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:46
msgid "``display``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:47
msgid "How the signature is shown, ``%s`` standing for it. By default, as written."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:49
msgid "``program``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:50
msgid "``true``: the name becomes the program that options described after it belong to, as ``.. program::`` makes it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:53
msgid "Other names for directives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:56
msgid "An extension may register one of Sphinx's own directives under another name:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:64
msgid "A target without a domain is a standard directive, or one of the default domain's; a target such as ``py:function`` names its domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:67
msgid "Migrating"
msgstr ""

#: ../../object-types.rst:70
msgid "``guidedog migrate`` finds each extension ``conf.py`` lists that is a module of the project, reads its ``add_crossref_type``, ``add_object_type``, and ``add_directive`` calls without running them, and writes these tables. It reports what it cannot declare: a ``parse_node`` function (set ``name``, ``display``, and ``program`` to match it) and directives, roles, and event handlers written in Python."
msgstr ""
